{"from": "site:capernaum", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-04-13-capernaum-home", "grade": "B", "note": "Excavations show Capernaum was a Hasmonean-period fishing village on the north-western shore of the lake, with basalt houses - the town the verse names, settled centuries before Jesus moved there."}
{"from": "site:capernaum", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-04-18-fishermen-called", "grade": "C", "note": "Fish hooks and weights for fish nets were found in the first-century town - the equipment of fishermen like Peter and Andrew."}
{"from": "site:capernaum-peter-house", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-04-13-capernaum-home", "grade": "B", "note": "A first-century dwelling under the octagonal church confirms domestic housing in Capernaum in the period."}
{"from": "site:capernaum-synagogue", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-04-23-teaching-synagogues", "grade": "D", "note": "The standing white synagogue is 4th-5th century; Loffreda suggests the basalt foundation beneath is the 1st-century synagogue, but others read the same floor as an open paved market area - the first-century date is disputed."}
{"from": "site:migdal-synagogue", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-04-23-teaching-synagogues", "grade": "B", "note": "A first-century synagogue at Magdala, discovered in a 2009 Israel Antiquities Authority salvage dig - one of only seven known synagogues from the Second Temple period - confirms Galilean synagogue worship in Jesus' lifetime."}
{"from": "site:magdala", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-04-18-fishermen-called", "grade": "C", "note": "Excavations at Magdala found a needle and lead weights for repairing and holding down fishing nets - the net fishing of the verse in a neighbouring shore town."}
{"from": "site:magdala", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:mt-04-18-fishermen-called", "note": "The identification of Magdala with Josephus's Tarichaea, whose Greek name derives from tarichos, 'fish preserved by salting or drying', remains disputed, so the fish-salting industry at this specific town cannot be secured from the fetched sources."}
{"from": "artifact:galilee-boat", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-04-18-fishermen-called", "grade": "C", "note": "A fishing boat from the 1st century AD, recovered from the Sea of Galilee in 1986 - the kind of boat Peter, Andrew, James and John worked from; the source states no evidence connects it to Jesus or his disciples."}
{"from": "artifact:galilee-boat", "type": "attests", "to": "place:sea-of-galilee", "grade": "C", "note": "An ancient fishing boat recovered from the lake itself confirms first-century navigation on the Sea of Galilee."}
{"from": "artifact:magdala-fishing-gear", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-04-18-fishermen-called", "grade": "C", "note": "A needle and lead weights for repairing and holding down fishing nets, excavated at Magdala - the nets and their repair of 4:18-21 as excavated equipment."}
{"from": "site:monastery-of-temptation", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-04-02-forty-days-fasting", "grade": "D", "note": "A 6th-century monastery built above the cave traditionally said to be where Jesus spent forty days fasting while tempted - it attests the commemorative tradition, not the event."}
{"from": "artifact:place-of-trumpeting", "type": "attests", "to": "place:temple-mount", "grade": "A", "note": "A 1st-century inscribed stone reading 'to the place of trumpeting', found in situ in 1968 in the excavations of the Temple Mount's southern wall by Benjamin Mazar."}
{"from": "artifact:place-of-trumpeting", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-04-05-pinnacle-of-temple", "grade": "B", "note": "The stone confirms the Herodian Temple Mount's corner parapets and the priests who trumpeted from them; it does not name the 'pinnacle' of the temptation."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-116-119", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-04-12-john-arrested", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus records that Herod (Antipas) slew John, called the Baptist, sending him a prisoner to Macherus, where he was put to death; Josephus ties the death to the war with Aretas (36 CE), later than Matthew's sequence."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-116-119", "type": "attests", "to": "person:john-the-baptist", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus names 'John, that was called the Baptist' and describes his teaching and baptism of crowds."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-116-119", "type": "attests", "to": "person:herod-antipas", "grade": "B", "note": "The passage names 'Herod, who had slain him' - Herod the tetrarch - and his execution of John at Macherus; the identification of that Herod with Herod Antipas, tetrarch of Galilee and Perea, rests on context, which Wikipedia (John the Baptist) states explicitly. Downgraded A to B by reviewer 1 (2026-11-11): the text names the tetrarch, not Antipas by name."}
{"from": "site:machaerus", "type": "attests", "to": "person:herod-antipas", "grade": "B", "note": "Upon Herod the Great's death the fortress passed to Herod Antipas, who ruled from 4 BC until 39 AD; John the Baptist was imprisoned and beheaded there during this time."}
{"from": "site:machaerus", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-04-12-john-arrested", "grade": "B", "note": "The excavated Herodian fortress east of the Dead Sea is the place where Josephus and the tradition locate John's imprisonment and beheading - the setting of 4:12's 'delivered up'. The site attests the event, not the person (retargeted from person:john-the-baptist by review 2 per the architect's site-to-person rule)."}
{"from": "site:machaerus", "type": "attests", "to": "place:beyond-the-jordan", "grade": "C", "note": "A fortress Herod the Great rebuilt as a base to safeguard his territories east of the Jordan - the 'beyond the Jordan' region of 4:25 and of John's later imprisonment (cf. Matt 11)."}
{"from": "artifact:antipas-coins", "type": "attests", "to": "person:herod-antipas", "grade": "B", "note": "Coins struck under Antipas are attested; Wikipedia notes his coins carried no images (aniconic), so they confirm his minting rule without naming him."}
{"from": "artifact:great-isaiah-scroll", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-04-15-isaiah-9-quoted", "grade": "B", "note": "The Great Isaiah Scroll, ca. 125 BCE, preserves the entire Book of Isaiah in Hebrew, including chapter 9, centuries before Matthew quoted it."}
